{
  "result": "Libraries have always felt like sanctuaries to me. Not the grand, echoing halls of famous institutions, but the smaller neighborhood ones with worn carpets and slightly crooked shelves. Walking through the doors, the scent of paper and dust greets you like an old friend. Fluorescent lights buzz softly overhead, and somewhere a page turns with a whisper.\n\nI remember spending entire afternoons lost between the stacks as a child. The world outside could wait. Inside, dragons soared, detectives solved impossible cases, and ordinary people discovered extraordinary courage. Each book was a portal, and I was free to step through as many as I liked.\n\nEven now, years later, I find myself drawn back. There's a particular comfort in browsing without purpose, letting titles catch my eye at random. A history of forgotten inventions. A novel about a lighthouse keeper. A collection of poems that make the heart ache in the best way. You never know what you'll find, and that uncertainty is part of the joy.\n\nIn an age of instant answers and endless scrolling, the library asks something different of us. It asks for patience. For curiosity without an agenda. For the willingness to sit with a story that unfolds at its own pace. And in return, it offers not just knowledge, but a kind of quiet companionship.\n\nSo I still go. I still wander the aisles. And every time, I leave carrying more than books. I leave carrying the reminder that wonder is always waiting, just a few shelves away.",
